{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"reasoning","text":"The existing tests pass. Now I need to analyze the clarity provider thoroughly and write focused tests for B6-014 that cover:\n\n1. Subject/action clarity\n2. Concrete nouns/verbs\n3. Unexplained jargon\n4. Ambiguous pronouns\n5. Nominalization\n6. Hedging\n7. Nested clauses\n8. Repeated qualifiers\n9. Buried instruction\n10. Missing context\n\nThe requirements say:\n- Focused tests prove every candidate includes intended audience and content role\n- Required/explained technical terms are not penalized\n- Specific labels are not treated as vague\n- Suggestions do not alter facts\n\n\n- Candidate-only behavior with no copy application\n- No churn if current behavior is already correct\n\nI'm examining the existing test file to understand the testing pattern. There are already three B6-014 tests covering basic ambiguity and hedges with audience binding, control-label contradictions, and a specific label test. I need to write additional focused tests that verify the existing behavior is correct and covers the full scope. My plan is to verify that ambiguous pronouns like \"It\" are properly flagged, unexplained jargon is caught, nominalizations are identified, and technical terms with explanations are protected from false positives.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific test cases I need to write: repeated hedges in a single sentence should flag the range, buried instructions should be caught, missing audience and purpose should trigger coverage gaps, ambiguous pronouns in a sentence with a clear antecedent in the same item should be properly evaluated, and multi-hedge sentences should only flag the repeated hedge pattern. I'm reviewing the exact test requirements to ensure I'm covering all the required patterns and edge cases. The tests need to verify that every candidate includes the intended audience and content role, that required or explained technical terms aren't flagged as vague, that specific labels stay as-is, and that suggestions preserve the original meaning. I should focus on testing the actual behavior rather than adding unnecessary churn, and I'll write a comprehensive test file that validates these constraints. Now I'm mapping out the specific detection patterns: ambiguous pronouns at the start, multiple hedges triggering the repeated-hedge rule, nominalizations like \"utilization\" that can be rephrased as actions, non-specific language like \"various\" and \"things\", passive constructions hiding the actor, nested clauses buried in complex sentences, and buried instructions where the action comes after conditions. Technical terms in the constraints should skip jargon and nominalization checks, so I need to test that edge case too. I'm mapping out test cases for each clarity issue type, making sure they cover the specific detection rules without overlapping. Now I need to verify that my technical term tests actually respect the constraints — checking whether the `technicalTerm` function properly recognizes jargon when it's in the `technicalUseTerms` or other term lists, so that flagged patterns like \"utilization\" won't trigger false positives on technical content. I'm seeing that hedging words like \"may\" are treated differently depending on context—when they're part of a technical term like \"authentication\", the nominalization regex catches the full word but the hedge check only looks at individual words like \"may\" in isolation, so I need to be careful about how I structure the test case to avoid false positives.\n\nLet me just run the existing tests to see what's actually failing, then I can write focused tests for the specific B6-014 behavior I'm checking for, making sure every candidate has the required audience and content role fields. I see the `technicalTerm` function pulls from multiple constraint arrays and checks if any term matches or contains the target word, plus there's a `technicalUse` boolean flag. The `requiredAndExplained` function validates that a term appears in both required and explained lists. I should focus on building a test file that systematically verifies each clarity detection path works correctly, including edge cases like unexplained jargon lists, specific labels without controls, and buried instructions with conditions. I'm tracing through how `hasCandidateContext` evaluates the item—it checks for an audience and that the content role isn't unknown. Since the item has an audience set to \"operators\" and the surface type is \"button\", the function returns true, allowing the candidate to be added. Now I need to verify the `technicalTerm` function properly handles this case, and then finalize the test implementation.\n\nI'm seeing that `technicalTerm` has some issues with substring matching—terms like \"nonce\" would match against \"authentication\" since one is contained within the other. This existing behavior is what the test expects, so I'll focus on writing comprehensive coverage that verifies both required and explained terms are properly excluded, and that unexplained jargon still gets flagged correctly.\n\nWriting test coverage... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... I'm checking whether the passive voice regex will match \"nonce is rotated\" — it's looking for a form of \"be\" followed by specific past participles, and \"rotated\" isn't in that list, so it won't trigger. This confirms that controlled passive constructions in technical contexts are correctly filtered out. For the unexplained jargon case, the term \"nonce\" would be checked against the technical terms list...\n\nChecking the technical terms logic... I see the issue now — `technicalTerm` returns true if the item has `technicalUse: true` or if any technical term matches, but my test only has a required term without that flag, so `technicalTerm('nonce')` would return false, allowing the candidate to be created as expected. I should also test that having `technicalUse: true` prevents candidates from being created even without required or explained terms, which covers that code path. Let me finalize the test cases and make sure I'm using the correct API and assertion methods.\n\nLooking at how the existing tests structure their content items, I notice I need to include the `binding` field consistently. I'm also realizing I should re-read the task requirements more carefully — the goal is to analyze subject/action clarity, concrete nouns and verbs, unexplained jargon, ambiguous pronouns, and other clarity issues, using role and audience context rather than applying universal thresholds.\n\nFor the deterministic control/outcome contradictions, I need exact spans and suggestions that preserve meaning while maintaining technical precision. The acceptance criteria emphasize that my tests should verify every candidate includes intended audience and content role, required technical terms aren't penalized, specific labels aren't treated as vague, and suggestions don't alter facts. I should also check that non-specific language detection works properly by testing a case with no matches, and I want to examine how the passive voice regex handles various constructions.\n\nTesting passive voice regex patterns...\n\nNow I need to think through what clarity issues each test case actually demonstrates and make sure my coverage is comprehensive. I'm realizing the existing test already covers the technical-clarity case, so I should focus my new tests on filling the gaps rather than duplicating what's there. Let me write a focused set of tests that cover the remaining scenarios without overcomplicating things. I'm realizing I've been overcomplicating this—there's already a `does-not-treat-specific-label-as-vague` test in the existing file, so I don't need to duplicate that. Let me just write focused tests for each clarity rule that actually exist in the code, making sure every candidate has both audience and content role. I'll also add a test for the `technicalUse: true` path to ensure those cases aren't incorrectly flagged as jargon. My plan is to cover the core behaviors: each candidate includes the required fields, technical terms aren't penalized, suggestions are editorial-only, and the technical use path works correctly.\n\nWriting the test file... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test assertions..."}}
